what an upsetting task you have had to  put this together i was listening to the  former health secretary jeremy hunt in  the house of commons a little earlier  who commissioned this report based  originally on 23 cases he said what has  been discovered during the course of  this has been beyond his darkest fears  talk to me about what you have uncovered  and the scale of it all  so as far as we're aware this is the  largest um  inquiry into a single service in the  history of the nhs  throughout the process i've been  supported by an amazing  multi-professional team of just over 90  midwives and doctors all with decades of  experience  we did start with a caseload of 23  families when we first reviewed those 23  cases we were of the unanimous view that  these were amongst the most serious  cases that we had seen in our careers  but of course once the news um got out  into the local system here in shropshire  that there was this uh independent  secretary of state uh review  the families just kept coming  we got to 60 we got 200 we got to 300  and then the numbers kept climbing so  what we've described today are the  reviews  1592 clinical incident reviews  undertaken by my expert independent team  it's hard for those numbers to sink in  as we said it started with 23 and so  many families draw into this  finding out about each other because i  would imagine when this happens to you  as a family you hope  that you  are the only ones but as you said as  this came out it was uncovered that this  went on over two decades at shrewsbury  and helford hospital trust  repeated failures what are the reasons  behind the repeated failures  so i think there are a range of reasons  first of all this was a trust that did  not investigate properly we know that  systems and structures of governance  clinical governance and investigation  has changed in our nhs over time and we  were careful to only ever judge the  trust against the accepted standards of  the time but each time we did that we  still found significant failings to  investigate uh failures to listen to  families and failure to learn  we came across action plans that even  when they were of a reasonable quality  and that wasn't often action plans that  were simply put on a shelf and  one of the things that my team  found most distressing  is that when they were reviewing cases  down the years down the line they kept  finding the same themes over and over  again  so my expert team of midwives and  doctors have said to me they've used  words like harrowing and distressing to  describe the work that they've needed to  do on behalf of the families in  shropshire  in this report you don't talk about  recommendations because whenever we hear  about a report that's been commissioned  into a scandal it talks about  recommendations you're very specific in  that you talk about essential and  immediate actions reading between the  lines you're basically saying this is so  bad this must not be the end of the  matter these changes have to be made  so there's no reading between the lines  at all we don't read between the lines  in our review team we say it how it is  these are you're absolutely right  immediate and essential actions or ieas  for short if you prefer the trust has  got more than 60 local actions for  learning and my team and i have an  absolute expectation that these are  delivered upon now that needs some  system changes the government has  announced a significant amount of  funding into maternity services um since  report one was published and further  funding again um in  before report two so that was that was  last week but we endorsed the findings  of the health select committee led by  jeremy hunt last year that said much  more funding needed to be required  needed to be provided and so we are  saying to the government that that  finding from the health select committee  must must must now happen  there's other immediate actions around  training around learning from situations  when mothers lose their lives trusts  have got to come together if they've all  delivered care  and you know there is just an awful lot  to do however we are confident that the  system the wider system from government  down to  um local regions down to trust boards  and wards  do understand the scale of the issue and  we will be watching carefully  to make sure that this does happen yeah  the trust have apologized today and they  have said that they have already started  work to make changes you mentioned those  60 specific actions for that particular  trust and 15  immediate and essential actions for all  maternity services in england  are we looking at a national crisis and  are you worried that services won't be  safe unless you get the level of funding  you've been talking about there which  the govern government haven't committed  to those levels of spending in this area  so i think the very positive thing that  has come out of what happened to so many  shropshire families  is that the level of scrutiny and  attention on maternity services is that  it is something that i've not seen in my  30-year career so it can feel difficult  when there's a harsh spotlight on a  particular service but i think much  positivity will come out of that i heard  the secretary of state stand up today to  say that he was accepting and endorsing  all of our immediate and essential  actions  that does mean  increased funding for workforce  that does mean  better standards seamless standards of  training across england  you know there is a lot to do but i am  confident that the attention on  maternity services is such that this  will now happen and happen at pace yeah  you have the health secretary support he  just needs the backing of the treasury  for funds talk to me about the families  involved i'm sure you'd like to pay  tribute to them today because we're  talking about mothers who have  campaigned  four years to get to this point and at  many points in the process i'm sure felt  like they were  a lone voice in the wind i think is how  i've heard um one family put it um can  you pay tribute to them today because  they're the ones that have got you to  this point and i'm sure they won't let  it up  absolutely what the first thing that i  did this morning as i launched um this  report was to pay tribute to the  families they were silenced for far too  long and many many families that the  quote about being a lone voice in the  wind came from the mummy of olivia  olivia died in 2002 and her mum appeared  on television in 2006 to try and get  someone to do something  it took the heroic efforts  of richard and rhiannon stanton davies  and kaylee and colin griffis after the  deaths of their babies kate in 2009 and  pippa in 2016 and to  get jeremy hunt who was then secretary  of state for health um to agree to this  uh review  there the cohort of families are known  as the secretary of state 23 the  original 23  um and they were made up of families who  had tried to get their voices heard for  so long  of course once the  news was out there that  this review had started the numbers  continued  to increase to where we got to today  and families have tried so hard they  described to me being exhausted by their  efforts to try to get their voices heard  but  they have done it we have done it and  now what we have thanks to those  families and my amazing review team is a  blueprint a safe road forward for  maternity services across england  yeah here's hoping that from this point  on family's voices are heard and it  leads to safer care nationwide and  senior midwife donna oxington thank you  so much for talking to us
 

